## 4. `${var%,}` cost — mechanism bench (M2)

One list, 1 800 000 bytes, 200 000 ids of 8 bytes joined by commas.

```
$ bash t/g.sh          # string built the way the base probe builds it
rep=1 one-strip=0.033 tr+cmdsub=0.055 y-strip=55.960 len=1799999/1799999
rep=2 one-strip=0.024 tr+cmdsub=0.062 y-strip=69.868 len=1799999/1799999

$ bash t/i.sh          # same string, three suffix-removal cases
len=1800000 last=,
nomatch-unquoted 68.568 len=1800000        # ${one%X}
nomatch-quoted   62.946 len=1800000        # ${one%"X"}
match-quoted      0.021 len=1799999        # ${one%","}

$ LC_ALL=<loc> bash t/t2.sh   # base shape: ids=$(cat nl-list) ; C_SERVED=$(printf|tr) ; ${C_SERVED%,}
locale=C.UTF-8     tr+cmdsub=0.045 strip=56.393
locale=en_US.UTF-8 tr+cmdsub=0.048 strip=65.086
locale=pt_BR.UTF-8 tr+cmdsub=0.025 strip=11.704    # locale absent on host -> C semantics
```

Read: the expansion costs 0.02 s when its pattern matches the last character and 56-69 s when it does
not (one scan per split point, multibyte-aware). The base probe's `ids=$(served_ids …)` strips the
trailing newline, so `tr '\n' ','` emits **no** trailing comma and the `%,` strip matched nothing: a
no-op that paid the scan. Independent in-situ confirmation: the F1 forgery (per-id fork removed)
still spends 39.38 s on T1 (`.ufo/waves/round-91/controls/c1-f1-count-cap.out`).

